Change: Remove unneeded code from the header stylesheet

core/css/header.css carried code for browsers the product no longer
supports. Eight declarations used the -ms-filter alpha syntax that only
IE 8 and 9 ever read, each sitting next to the standard opacity every
current browser uses. The app name container declared display in the
-webkit-flex, -ms-flexbox and -moz-box variants of Chrome 18, IE 11 and
Firefox 14 - all three overridden by the plain display: flex that
followed them, and all three already marked for removal in the file
itself. The navigation rule kept two commented-out overflow declarations
with no reason recorded anywhere for switching them off.

Two hover backgrounds passed their alpha channel to rgb() instead of
rgba(). Current browsers accept the four-argument form of rgb(), but
anything older than Chrome 65 or Firefox 52 drops such a declaration
entirely and would have rendered the hover without its background, while
the rest of the file uses rgba() throughout.

None of this changes what is drawn. The stylesheet parses to the same
rules and declarations as before, with identical computed values.
